# .age-recipients
# One age PUBLIC key per line -- one line per machine that must be able to
# decrypt .env.sops.yaml. Blank lines and # comments are ignored.
#
# TRACKED IN GIT BY DESIGN. These are PUBLIC keys: publishing them grants
# nothing. The matching PRIVATE identity never leaves the machine that made it
# and lives at ~/.config/sops/age/keys.txt (mode 600), never in this repo.
#
# ADD A MACHINE
#   1. On that machine:  age-keygen -o ~/.config/sops/age/keys.txt
#      The PUBLIC key is printed on stderr as: Public key: age1...
#   2. Append it below with a comment naming the host.
#   3. pnpm run env:recipients     regenerates .sops.yaml
#   4. pnpm run env:encrypt        re-encrypts to the new recipient set
#   5. Commit .age-recipients, .sops.yaml and .env.sops.yaml together.
#
# REVOKE A MACHINE
#   Delete its line, repeat steps 3-5, then ROTATE the underlying credentials.
#   Removing a recipient stops FUTURE decryption; it does not retroactively
#   protect ciphertext that machine already held. Git history keeps the old
#   blob, so revocation without rotation is theatre.
#
# ROSTER STATUS IS NOT PROSE. env-bootstrap-roster.guard.test.ts asserts that
# .sops.yaml is a byte-identical render of the keys below, so the two files
# cannot drift. A machine is a recipient if and only if its key appears here;
# no comment, table or status word grants access. The previous revision of this
# header carried a status column marking two machines as not-yet-added, which
# read as a roster of three while exactly one key was listed -- prose doing the
# job of an assertion.
